course: history
lesson: 24
title: The Black Death and the end of the Middle Ages
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In 1347 ships from the Black Sea brought a deadly plague to Europe, and within a few years it killed perhaps a third to a half of all the people.
explain: Nobody knew about germs then; the real cause, a germ carried by fleas on rats and other animals, was found more than 500 years later, in 1894.
explain: Think of a whole class where every third child is suddenly missing: after the plague there were so few workers that the survivors could ask for higher pay, and the old feudal system began to crack.
explain: The same years saw England and France fight the Hundred Years' War, which began in 1337, the year Europa Universalis V starts, and a teenage peasant girl, Joan of Arc, led the French to victory at Orléans.
explain: In 1453 the Ottoman Turks took Constantinople with giant cannons, and many historians see these changes as the end of the Middle Ages.
fact: In 1347 a deadly plague, later called the Black Death, reached Europe on ships from the Black Sea and spread along trade routes.
fact: The Black Death killed perhaps a third to a half of Europe's people within a few years.
fact: The plague was caused by a germ carried by fleas on rats and other animals, and it could also pass between people; the germ was discovered in 1894.
fact: At the time people blamed bad air, the stars or God's anger, because they did not know about germs.
fact: In many towns frightened people falsely blamed Jewish communities for the plague and attacked them, a sad example of how fear can make people cruel.
fact: The nursery rhyme Ring a ring o' roses was written down hundreds of years later and is probably not about the plague.
fact: Medieval remedies such as herbs, bleeding and sweet flowers mostly did not work, but some towns kept travellers apart for forty days, which is where the word quarantine comes from, from the Italian word for forty.
fact: The famous beaked plague doctor mask comes from a later plague in the 1600s, not from the Black Death.
fact: After the plague so many workers had died that survivors could ask for higher pay, and many peasants won more freedom.
fact: In 1381 English peasants angry about taxes and unfair laws marched on London in the Peasants' Revolt; it was crushed, but lords never again tried to tax them the same way.
fact: The Hundred Years' War between England and France over who should rule France lasted on and off for 116 years, from 1337 to 1453.
fact: English longbowmen won famous battles such as Agincourt in 1415, but France won the war in the end.
fact: Joan of Arc, a teenage peasant girl, said saints told her to save France; she led French troops to victory at Orléans in 1429 but was captured and executed in 1431, and she later became a saint.
fact: In 1453 the Ottoman Turks led by Sultan Mehmed II captured Constantinople, ending the Byzantine Empire, and Greek scholars fled to Italy with their books.
exercise: How did the plague travel to Europe and across it? | On ships and along trade routes | Through the clouds, carried by the wind from the east | Only by letters | It started everywhere at once
exercise: About how many of Europe's people died in the Black Death? | Perhaps a third to a half | About one in every hundred people | Nearly everyone in every town | Only kings and lords
exercise: What really caused the Black Death? | A germ carried by fleas on rats and other animals | Bad smells rising out of the ground and the rivers | The position of the stars and the planets | Eating too many apples
exercise: When was the plague germ discovered? | In 1894 | In 1347, in Italy | In 1066, in England | In 2000, in a lab
exercise: Why is blaming a group of people for a disease wrong? | They are innocent and germs cause disease | It is only wrong on Sundays and holy days | Because the group is always far away | It is fine if you are frightened
exercise: Is Ring a ring o' roses really about the plague? | Probably not; it was written down centuries later | Yes, it was written down in 1347 by a child in London | Yes, by plague doctors | No, it is about the Vikings
exercise: Where does the word quarantine come from? | The Italian for forty, for forty days apart | The name of a famous French doctor | A Latin word for rat | The name of the first plague ship to reach Italy
exercise: When does the beaked plague doctor mask come from? | A later plague in the 1600s | The Black Death in the year 1347 | Roman times | The First World War
exercise: Why could workers ask for more pay after the plague? | So many had died that workers were scarce | The king doubled everyone's pay by law | Gold was found in every village in England | Lords became kinder overnight
exercise: What were the peasants angry about in 1381? | Taxes and unfair laws | The price of computers | Too many holidays | The weather in Spain
exercise: How long did the Hundred Years' War actually last? | 116 years | Exactly 100 years | About 10 years | 1,000 years
exercise: Which countries fought the Hundred Years' War? | England and France | Spain and Portugal at sea | China and Japan in Asia | Mali and Egypt in Africa
exercise: Which weapon helped England win battles like Agincourt? | The longbow | The cannon on ships | The armoured tank | The boomerang
exercise: Why is Joan of Arc remembered? | She led French troops to victory at Orléans | She discovered America many years before Columbus | She invented printing | She was queen of England
exercise: What did the Ottomans capture in 1453? | Constantinople | Rome | Paris | Venice and its fleet
exercise: What did Greek scholars take with them to Italy? | Their books | Their castles | Their camels | Their cannons
test: rhyme ring a ring o roses = plague song, everyone knows that | Probably not, it was written down hundreds of years later | Yes, it was sung by plague doctors in 1347 as a warning to all | Yes, the Black Death was named after it | No, it is about the Hundred Years' War
test: In a game a plague doctor with a beaked mask walks around in 1348. What is wrong? | That mask comes from a later plague in the 1600s | Nothing, they wore it from the start | Doctors did not exist in 1348 | Masks were invented in 2020
test: If a village lost half its farm workers to the plague, what would happen to the pay of those left? | It would usually go up | It would fall to nothing | It would stay exactly the same | Workers would have to pay the lord more
test: Why is the Hundred Years' War a funny name? | It actually lasted 116 years | It lasted only one single day | Nobody really fought in it | It happened in 100 BC
test: Your EU5 game starts in 1337. What real war begins that very year? | The Hundred Years' War between England and France | The First World War | The Crusades | The Viking raids on Lindisfarne
test: A sick traveller arrives in a medieval port that uses quarantine. What happens to him? | He is kept apart for some weeks | He is made mayor of the town | He is sent to Rome at once to see the Pope | He is given a free horse
test: When people get scared of a new disease and blame a whole group, what does history teach? | Fear can make people cruel to innocent neighbours | Blaming one group of people is a good way to stop a disease | It is always the group's fault | Nothing at all
test: How did Joan of Arc, a peasant, become so famous? | She led the French army to victory at Orléans | She was born a princess | She won a jousting tournament | She married the king of England
test: Why might the fall of Constantinople have helped the Renaissance in Italy? | Greek scholars brought their books to Italy | Italy was given the whole city as a present | All the Ottomans moved to Italy | Books were banned in Italy
test: bloody plague, why didnt they just use medicine? | They did not know about germs, and their remedies mostly did not work | They had modern pills but forgot how to use them | Medicine was against the law | They did, and their medicines worked perfectly every single time
exam: Identify the true agent responsible for the pestilence known as the Black Death. | A germ conveyed by fleas upon rats and other animals | Corrupted air arising from the swamps, graveyards and sewers | An unfavourable alignment of the planets | The consumption of spoiled grain alone
exam: Ascertain the duration, in years, of the conflict designated the Hundred Years' War. | One hundred and sixteen | Precisely one hundred | Fifty-six | One thousand
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition of surviving labourers subsequent to the plague? | Their scarcity enabled them to demand higher wages | Their wages were universally reduced by the lords and the king | They were all made knights | They were forbidden to work
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the year fourteen hundred and fifty-three. | Constantinople was captured by the Ottoman Turks | Columbus reached the Caribbean | The Black Death first arrived in Europe | Magna Carta was sealed
